  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nExtreme Heat Watch Sunday - Wednesday: Ensemble guidance is in\nagreement that the upper level ridge out west will\nelongate/spread east late weekend through early next week.\nRising heights and increasing 850mb temps will allow for temps\nto increase well back into the 90s. NBM interquartile ranges are\nabout 3-5 degrees with many areas reaching the lower to upper\n90s. Locations west of Springfield have not seen as much rain\nlately and those areas could see highs approach 100 by Tuesday\nand Wednesday.\n\nThose temps alone are not unusual for this time of year however\nwith dews staying in the upper 60s to lower 70s, this will push\nheat indicies up to 100 degrees with some areas around 105\ndegrees for several days. This creates a prolonged heat risk.\nSo far many times this summer we have seen highest afternoon \ndewpoints and heat indicies off of the higher terrain of the \nOzark Plateau. We have seen deeper mixing on the plateau with \nheat indices staying a tad lower. Currently the highest \nconfidence area for heat indicies to reach 105 is in the area \nthat we will be issuing an Extreme Heat Watch for Sunday through\nWednesday from roughly a corridor from the Joplin metro up \ntowards the Lake of the Ozarks region. What may occur is that an\neventual Heat Advisory may be needed for most of the area with \nsome areas getting into a Extreme Heat Warning. Future updates \nwill expand on this possibility. \n\nAlso of note is that the NWS Experimental Heat Risk tool does\nshow a large portion of the area in the major category which\nmeans that heat related impacts affect anyone with\ncooling/hydration as well as health systems and industries. \n\nRain Chances Return Mid/Late Week: Ensemble cluster analysis\nthen shows the ridge flattening out a bit with a return to a\nslightly northwest flow aloft which will allow for a return to\nprecip chances. Currently rain chances are highest north of I-44\nhowever confidence in placement of rain chances at this range in\nthat type of pattern is low and would expect additional changes\nwith future updates. NBM temps spreads are much larger by late\nnext week which also leads to a lower confidence time frame.",
